<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- Google OAuth clients, project 486134999809. Client IDs are public
         identifiers, not secrets, so they are safe to keep here.

         ANDROID client: 486134999809-2r9g1i1mne2gtronblrkrpc1p7l7qn7n.apps.googleusercontent.com
         WEB client:     486134999809-gshnt5i4kmlga2bia5jvem7c14p5n6c3.apps.googleusercontent.com

         Only the WEB one is ever passed to code — GetSignInWithGoogleOption on the
         client (BuildConfig.GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID) and GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID on the
         server, which must match, because Google mints ID tokens whose `aud` is the
         web client even on Android.

         The ANDROID client is never referenced in source, but Credential Manager
         still requires it to exist and to list this app's package plus the signing
         certificate SHA-1 — Play Services validates the caller against it. A missing
         or mismatched SHA-1 fails as ConnectionResult{statusCode=DEVELOPER_ERROR},
         which surfaces only in logcat: the account picker still opens, then closes
         with no token and no HTTP request.

         ⚠️ An Android OAuth client holds exactly ONE package name + ONE SHA-1, and
         the debug keystore is PER MACHINE. So supporting N dev machines means N
         Android clients in the project — same package name, different SHA-1 — all
         of which work with the single WEB client ID above. Known fingerprints:
             CF:C1:4C:98:B2:0C:BE:DF:0F:E6:A9:69:D1:66:F1:7F:F6:56:3A:08  desktop
             D2:86:8C:A0:3B:C7:7D:AB:6C:0D:F1:6F:42:76:46:0F:A8:37:C6:34  laptop
         Read a machine's own with:
             keytool -list -v -keystore ~/.android/debug.keystore \
                 -alias androiddebugkey -storepass android -keypass android
         Read what a build ACTUALLY shipped (the reliable check, since the installed
         APK may not be the local one):
             apksigner verify (with the print-certs flag) on the pulled APK
         Console changes take five minutes to a few hours to propagate. -->

    <application
        android:name=".ForgeApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Forge">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Forge">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>